1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,960
Meet Mel.

2
00:00:01,960 --> 00:00:03,331
In our last episode,

3
00:00:03,331 --> 00:00:04,921
she tried to buy a toaster that

4
00:00:04,921 --> 00:00:06,094
wasn't made in China,

5
00:00:06,094 --> 00:00:07,270
and failed.

6
00:00:07,270 --> 00:00:08,827
So now she wants to build one

7
00:00:08,827 --> 00:00:09,473
herself,

8
00:00:09,473 --> 00:00:10,740
in Ohio.

9
00:00:10,970 --> 00:00:13,900
Picture her factory on a Sunday afternoon.

10
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:15,460
Robot arms working.

11
00:00:15,460 --> 00:00:17,500
Cameras checking every part.

12
00:00:17,500 --> 00:00:20,110
Software deciding what to build next.

13
00:00:20,110 --> 00:00:22,960
And a price that matches China's.

14
00:00:22,990 --> 00:00:25,320
That factory doesn't exist yet.

15
00:00:25,320 --> 00:00:27,646
But almost every piece of it already

16
00:00:27,646 --> 00:00:28,400
does.

17
00:00:28,400 --> 00:00:30,813
So could AI make American factories

18
00:00:30,813 --> 00:00:32,380
cheaper than China's?

19
00:00:32,380 --> 00:00:33,412
And if it did,

20
00:00:33,412 --> 00:00:35,907
would America actually win?

21
00:00:46,550 --> 00:00:48,940
Mel's banker isn't convinced.

22
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:49,893
For forty years,

23
00:00:49,893 --> 00:00:51,610
the rule was simple.

24
00:00:51,610 --> 00:00:53,860
Build where the work is cheapest.

25
00:00:53,900 --> 00:00:56,480
But a robot doesn't care about wages.

26
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:58,443
If machines do more of the work,

27
00:00:58,443 --> 00:01:00,600
that old rule starts to wobble.

28
00:01:00,600 --> 00:01:02,261
And the stakes are bigger than one

29
00:01:02,261 --> 00:01:03,210
toaster.

30
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:03,860
Prices,

31
00:01:03,860 --> 00:01:04,285
jobs,

32
00:01:04,285 --> 00:01:06,077
and which country makes the things

33
00:01:06,077 --> 00:01:08,021
everybody needs.

34
00:01:08,283 --> 00:01:12,133
And America has already seen a preview.

35
00:01:12,193 --> 00:01:12,800
Today,

36
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:15,309
American factories make about twice as

37
00:01:15,309 --> 00:01:16,773
much as they did in the late

38
00:01:16,773 --> 00:01:17,589
seventies,

39
00:01:17,589 --> 00:01:19,653
with millions fewer workers.

40
00:01:19,703 --> 00:01:20,463
More stuff,

41
00:01:20,463 --> 00:01:22,373
far fewer people.

42
00:01:22,423 --> 00:01:24,485
Automation is a big part of that

43
00:01:24,485 --> 00:01:25,497
story.

44
00:01:29,750 --> 00:01:31,725
So Mel calls her old friend Wei,

45
00:01:31,725 --> 00:01:34,700
the line engineer from our last episode,

46
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:36,950
Why the World Still Depends on Chinese

47
00:01:36,950 --> 00:01:38,270
Factories.

48
00:01:38,270 --> 00:01:39,202
Wei's answer,

49
00:01:39,202 --> 00:01:40,280
in short.

50
00:01:40,310 --> 00:01:43,263
China makes about a quarter of everything

51
00:01:43,263 --> 00:01:44,929
the world manufactures,

52
00:01:44,929 --> 00:01:46,385
with suppliers down the street,

53
00:01:46,385 --> 00:01:46,996
giant ports,

54
00:01:46,996 --> 00:01:48,030
steady power,

55
00:01:48,030 --> 00:01:50,739
and engineers who've done it all before.

56
00:01:50,983 --> 00:01:51,913
And labor?

57
00:01:51,913 --> 00:01:52,823
By our rough math,

58
00:01:52,823 --> 00:01:55,239
an American factory worker earns about

59
00:01:55,239 --> 00:01:56,911
five times what Wei's line workers make

60
00:01:56,911 --> 00:01:57,883
per hour.

61
00:01:58,173 --> 00:02:00,130
That gap is why Mel's old toaster

62
00:02:00,130 --> 00:02:02,087
was made in China in the first

63
00:02:02,087 --> 00:02:02,931
place.

64
00:02:03,183 --> 00:02:05,832
So Mel's whole plan rests on a

65
00:02:05,832 --> 00:02:06,713
bet.

66
00:02:06,713 --> 00:02:08,948
If robots shrink the labor part of

67
00:02:08,948 --> 00:02:09,668
the bill,

68
00:02:09,668 --> 00:02:12,503
does China's oldest head start shrink too?

69
00:02:12,593 --> 00:02:13,041
First,

70
00:02:13,041 --> 00:02:14,691
she needs to know what AI can

71
00:02:14,691 --> 00:02:17,412
really do on a factory floor.

72
00:02:21,650 --> 00:02:22,176
Kip,

73
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:23,532
her consultant,

74
00:02:23,532 --> 00:02:26,070
starts the tour with what's proven.

75
00:02:26,350 --> 00:02:29,510
Cameras running AI software inspect every

76
00:02:29,510 --> 00:02:31,917
toaster faster than a human eye,

77
00:02:31,917 --> 00:02:33,862
and guide robot arms to grab parts

78
00:02:33,862 --> 00:02:36,650
that aren't perfectly lined up.

79
00:02:37,090 --> 00:02:40,072
The International Federation of Robotics,

80
00:02:40,072 --> 00:02:42,054
the industry's statistics body,

81
00:02:42,054 --> 00:02:44,455
calls machine vision industry-grade.

82
00:02:44,717 --> 00:02:45,208
Next,

83
00:02:45,208 --> 00:02:47,237
predictive maintenance.

84
00:02:47,237 --> 00:02:49,206
Sensors listen to a motor,

85
00:02:49,206 --> 00:02:51,797
and software flags it before it breaks.

86
00:02:51,897 --> 00:02:54,064
Smart scheduling reshuffles the line when

87
00:02:54,064 --> 00:02:55,747
an order changes.

88
00:02:56,007 --> 00:02:58,392
Self-driving carts move parts across the

89
00:02:58,392 --> 00:02:59,257
floor.

90
00:02:59,487 --> 00:03:01,573
The federation says tools like these are

91
00:03:01,573 --> 00:03:04,259
increasingly used in industry.

92
00:03:04,517 --> 00:03:06,761
Then Kip shows her a digital twin,

93
00:03:06,761 --> 00:03:09,254
a virtual copy of a factory where

94
00:03:09,254 --> 00:03:11,520
engineers test the line before they build

95
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:12,347
it.

96
00:03:12,427 --> 00:03:14,820
The electronics giant Foxconn says it

97
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:16,974
built one with NVIDIA software for a

98
00:03:16,974 --> 00:03:19,103
new plant in Guadalajara,

99
00:03:19,103 --> 00:03:20,036
Mexico,

100
00:03:20,036 --> 00:03:23,269
to train its robots in simulation first.

101
00:03:23,517 --> 00:03:26,057
Let me sort what Mel just saw.

102
00:03:26,087 --> 00:03:26,779
Proven,

103
00:03:26,779 --> 00:03:28,424
the robot arms that weld,

104
00:03:28,424 --> 00:03:29,354
paint and pick,

105
00:03:29,354 --> 00:03:31,087
plus machine vision.

106
00:03:31,087 --> 00:03:31,738
Growing,

107
00:03:31,738 --> 00:03:33,287
predictive maintenance,

108
00:03:33,287 --> 00:03:35,887
AI inspection and smart scheduling.

109
00:03:35,887 --> 00:03:36,488
Not yet,

110
00:03:36,488 --> 00:03:38,975
general-purpose robots that learn any job

111
00:03:38,975 --> 00:03:40,127
by watching.

112
00:03:40,127 --> 00:03:42,684
The federation says that kind of physical

113
00:03:42,684 --> 00:03:44,776
AI isn't industry-grade yet.

114
00:03:45,017 --> 00:03:47,730
Mel asks about the humanoid robots from

115
00:03:47,730 --> 00:03:49,267
viral videos.

116
00:03:49,267 --> 00:03:51,191
The federation says most are still

117
00:03:51,191 --> 00:03:53,308
prototypes or pilot projects,

118
00:03:53,308 --> 00:03:55,424
and that for factory work today,

119
00:03:55,424 --> 00:03:58,203
ordinary industrial robots are the better

120
00:03:58,203 --> 00:03:59,017
deal.

121
00:03:59,227 --> 00:04:01,304
The robot that matters isn't the one

122
00:04:01,304 --> 00:04:02,437
that dances.

123
00:04:02,437 --> 00:04:05,358
It's the arm bolted to the floor.

124
00:04:05,617 --> 00:04:07,666
And here's why that arm matters less

125
00:04:07,666 --> 00:04:09,177
than Mel hoped.

126
00:04:09,377 --> 00:04:11,224
In American factories,

127
00:04:11,224 --> 00:04:13,486
out of every dollar of sales,

128
00:04:13,486 --> 00:04:15,769
only about thirteen cents go to wages

129
00:04:15,769 --> 00:04:17,617
and salaries for everyone,

130
00:04:17,617 --> 00:04:19,877
from the line to the front office.

131
00:04:20,047 --> 00:04:22,349
Materials and parts take by far the

132
00:04:22,349 --> 00:04:23,717
biggest bite.

133
00:04:23,950 --> 00:04:26,569
Watch what happens when I pull labor

134
00:04:26,569 --> 00:04:27,450
out.

135
00:04:27,450 --> 00:04:29,795
Even if every paycheck vanished,

136
00:04:29,795 --> 00:04:30,760
the materials,

137
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:31,726
the machines,

138
00:04:31,726 --> 00:04:33,696
the energy and the building would still

139
00:04:33,696 --> 00:04:34,670
be there.

140
00:04:34,670 --> 00:04:36,475
And the robots that replace those

141
00:04:36,475 --> 00:04:38,880
paychecks send a bill of their own.

142
00:04:39,150 --> 00:04:41,640
Automation shrinks a slice.

143
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:43,895
It doesn't make the factory free.

144
00:04:48,150 --> 00:04:50,580
So Mel runs an experiment.

145
00:04:50,580 --> 00:04:52,486
Her toaster costs a hundred dollars to

146
00:04:52,486 --> 00:04:54,774
make in a regular Chinese factory and

147
00:04:54,774 --> 00:04:57,280
ship to an American warehouse.

148
00:04:57,420 --> 00:04:59,211
Our model is illustrative,

149
00:04:59,211 --> 00:05:01,330
not any real company's costs.

150
00:05:01,330 --> 00:05:03,854
What matters is how it moves.

151
00:05:04,117 --> 00:05:04,595
First,

152
00:05:04,595 --> 00:05:07,047
the conventional Chinese plant.

153
00:05:07,047 --> 00:05:07,930
Cheap labor,

154
00:05:07,930 --> 00:05:09,040
suppliers next door,

155
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:10,376
an ocean to cross,

156
00:05:10,376 --> 00:05:12,697
and a tariff at the border.

157
00:05:12,697 --> 00:05:14,785
That's Mel's benchmark.

158
00:05:15,050 --> 00:05:15,533
Next,

159
00:05:15,533 --> 00:05:16,271
Kip's dream,

160
00:05:16,271 --> 00:05:19,670
a highly automated plant in Ohio.

161
00:05:19,680 --> 00:05:21,733
Robots cut most of the labor hours,

162
00:05:21,733 --> 00:05:24,200
but American pay is higher.

163
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:24,889
Robots,

164
00:05:24,889 --> 00:05:27,198
software and cameras add a big bill

165
00:05:27,198 --> 00:05:28,490
of their own.

166
00:05:28,570 --> 00:05:30,508
No ocean and no tariff on the

167
00:05:30,508 --> 00:05:31,132
toaster,

168
00:05:31,132 --> 00:05:33,670
but many parts still come from Asia.

169
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:34,690
The total?

170
00:05:34,690 --> 00:05:36,635
Just two dollars more.

171
00:05:36,883 --> 00:05:38,323
Almost a tie.

172
00:05:38,323 --> 00:05:39,933
That's the headline.

173
00:05:39,963 --> 00:05:41,253
In a model like this,

174
00:05:41,253 --> 00:05:43,870
automation can bring an American factory

175
00:05:43,870 --> 00:05:46,153
within a few dollars of China.

176
00:05:46,243 --> 00:05:48,763
Now watch what moves the winner.

177
00:05:49,017 --> 00:05:50,939
Raise the tariff a lot,

178
00:05:50,939 --> 00:05:53,767
and the Chinese plant gets pricier.

179
00:05:53,787 --> 00:05:55,587
Ohio wins.

180
00:05:55,627 --> 00:05:58,199
Now let Ohio's robots sit idle half

181
00:05:58,199 --> 00:06:00,857
the time because orders are weak.

182
00:06:01,107 --> 00:06:03,342
The cost of the machines behind every

183
00:06:03,342 --> 00:06:04,677
toaster doubles.

184
00:06:04,677 --> 00:06:06,437
China wins again.

185
00:06:06,607 --> 00:06:07,375
Tariffs,

186
00:06:07,375 --> 00:06:08,671
busy robots,

187
00:06:08,671 --> 00:06:09,968
imported parts,

188
00:06:09,968 --> 00:06:11,437
robot prices.

189
00:06:11,437 --> 00:06:13,427
Those are the switches.

190
00:06:13,683 --> 00:06:16,823
Then Wei calls back with another option.

191
00:06:16,823 --> 00:06:18,064
The same robots,

192
00:06:18,064 --> 00:06:19,423
in China.

193
00:06:19,453 --> 00:06:20,729
Lower wages,

194
00:06:20,729 --> 00:06:22,266
suppliers down the street,

195
00:06:22,266 --> 00:06:25,132
and Chinese-made robots that we assume

196
00:06:25,132 --> 00:06:26,233
cost less.

197
00:06:26,233 --> 00:06:28,937
It comes in about five dollars under

198
00:06:28,937 --> 00:06:30,275
Mel's benchmark.

199
00:06:30,517 --> 00:06:31,920
In our base case,

200
00:06:31,920 --> 00:06:35,097
Wei's automated factory wins.

201
00:06:35,217 --> 00:06:38,097
Ohio only comes out ahead when tariffs

202
00:06:38,097 --> 00:06:40,857
are high and its robots stay busy.

203
00:06:41,227 --> 00:06:43,607
So automation doesn't automatically favor

204
00:06:43,607 --> 00:06:44,887
America.

205
00:06:44,997 --> 00:06:47,216
It favors whoever automates best,

206
00:06:47,216 --> 00:06:48,427
at scale.

207
00:06:48,657 --> 00:06:50,797
Which raises the real question.

208
00:06:50,797 --> 00:06:53,564
Is China actually automating?

209
00:06:57,817 --> 00:06:58,917
It is.

210
00:06:58,917 --> 00:06:59,556
Last year,

211
00:06:59,556 --> 00:07:01,761
factories in China installed more

212
00:07:01,761 --> 00:07:03,965
industrial robots than the rest of the

213
00:07:03,965 --> 00:07:05,447
world combined.

214
00:07:05,507 --> 00:07:07,142
Compared with America,

215
00:07:07,142 --> 00:07:08,979
that's roughly nine robots for every

216
00:07:08,979 --> 00:07:10,497
American one.

217
00:07:10,537 --> 00:07:12,212
In a single year.

218
00:07:12,450 --> 00:07:14,070
Let me chart it.

219
00:07:14,130 --> 00:07:17,090
China's tower dwarfs everyone else.

220
00:07:17,090 --> 00:07:18,084
America,

221
00:07:18,084 --> 00:07:19,890
now the second-biggest market,

222
00:07:19,890 --> 00:07:21,910
looks like a stub next to it.

223
00:07:21,980 --> 00:07:22,847
And worldwide,

224
00:07:22,847 --> 00:07:24,825
installations hit a record,

225
00:07:24,825 --> 00:07:27,048
according to the federation's World

226
00:07:27,048 --> 00:07:28,760
Robotics report.

227
00:07:29,017 --> 00:07:30,667
Second surprise.

228
00:07:30,667 --> 00:07:32,474
More and more of those robots are

229
00:07:32,474 --> 00:07:33,977
made in China.

230
00:07:34,087 --> 00:07:35,864
A few years ago,

231
00:07:35,864 --> 00:07:38,575
foreign brands sold most of China's new

232
00:07:38,575 --> 00:07:39,547
robots.

233
00:07:39,547 --> 00:07:42,332
Now Chinese brands sell more than half.

234
00:07:42,583 --> 00:07:45,363
Now the twist inside the twist.

235
00:07:45,363 --> 00:07:46,820
Per factory worker,

236
00:07:46,820 --> 00:07:49,743
China isn't the most automated country.

237
00:07:49,803 --> 00:07:52,279
America has more robots per worker,

238
00:07:52,279 --> 00:07:54,567
and South Korea has several times more

239
00:07:54,567 --> 00:07:55,583
than both.

240
00:07:55,883 --> 00:07:58,472
China simply has so many factory workers

241
00:07:58,472 --> 00:08:00,045
that its robots are spread thin,

242
00:08:00,045 --> 00:08:00,923
for now.

243
00:08:00,923 --> 00:08:02,027
That's room to grow.

244
00:08:02,283 --> 00:08:04,693
And it has reasons to grow.

245
00:08:04,903 --> 00:08:07,940
The federation points to a labor shortage

246
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:10,192
from an aging population,

247
00:08:10,192 --> 00:08:12,469
and China's new five-year plan aims AI

248
00:08:12,469 --> 00:08:15,853
research at physical uses like robots.

249
00:08:15,903 --> 00:08:18,973
Robots don't cancel China's advantages.

250
00:08:19,003 --> 00:08:21,275
Plugged into its supplier network,

251
00:08:21,275 --> 00:08:23,253
they may multiply them.

252
00:08:27,517 --> 00:08:29,067
Back in Ohio,

253
00:08:29,067 --> 00:08:32,197
Mel hits the supply-chain problem.

254
00:08:32,387 --> 00:08:34,889
Her factory can be brand new and

255
00:08:34,889 --> 00:08:37,127
still depend on the world.

256
00:08:37,127 --> 00:08:38,190
Robot arms,

257
00:08:38,190 --> 00:08:39,525
precision gears,

258
00:08:39,525 --> 00:08:40,316
sensors,

259
00:08:40,316 --> 00:08:40,834
chips,

260
00:08:40,834 --> 00:08:43,832
and the rare-earth magnets inside powerful

261
00:08:43,832 --> 00:08:44,977
motors.

262
00:08:45,127 --> 00:08:47,298
Build the factory at home,

263
00:08:47,298 --> 00:08:49,221
and you may still buy its brain

264
00:08:49,221 --> 00:08:51,744
and its muscles from abroad.

265
00:08:51,983 --> 00:08:54,462
And those suppliers live in different

266
00:08:54,462 --> 00:08:55,453
countries.

267
00:08:55,453 --> 00:08:57,059
Japan builds more of the world's

268
00:08:57,059 --> 00:08:59,633
industrial robots than anyone else.

269
00:08:59,633 --> 00:09:02,008
Germany and Switzerland are home to big

270
00:09:02,008 --> 00:09:03,833
automation suppliers.

271
00:09:03,833 --> 00:09:06,465
America leads in AI software and chip

272
00:09:06,465 --> 00:09:07,413
design.

273
00:09:07,413 --> 00:09:09,463
Taiwan and South Korea make many of

274
00:09:09,463 --> 00:09:10,343
the chips.

275
00:09:10,343 --> 00:09:12,520
And China refines most of the rare

276
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:14,426
earths behind those magnets.

277
00:09:14,683 --> 00:09:16,548
Let me draw where the money from

278
00:09:16,548 --> 00:09:18,153
Mel's toaster could go.

279
00:09:18,153 --> 00:09:19,723
Materials and parts.

280
00:09:19,723 --> 00:09:20,993
The robot maker.

281
00:09:20,993 --> 00:09:22,353
Chips and sensors.

282
00:09:22,353 --> 00:09:23,653
AI software.

283
00:09:23,653 --> 00:09:24,443
Energy.

284
00:09:24,443 --> 00:09:25,613
Logistics.

285
00:09:25,613 --> 00:09:26,815
And whatever is left,

286
00:09:26,815 --> 00:09:28,634
for the factory owner.

287
00:09:28,883 --> 00:09:30,548
The splits in our picture are

288
00:09:30,548 --> 00:09:31,714
illustrative,

289
00:09:31,714 --> 00:09:33,503
but the lesson isn't.

290
00:09:33,633 --> 00:09:35,627
The country that hosts the factory may

291
00:09:35,627 --> 00:09:37,623
not capture most of the value.

292
00:09:37,693 --> 00:09:39,485
In an automated world,

293
00:09:39,485 --> 00:09:41,957
money flows to whoever owns the robots,

294
00:09:41,957 --> 00:09:43,918
the software and the chips.

295
00:09:48,183 --> 00:09:48,630
So,

296
00:09:48,630 --> 00:09:51,456
could AI make American factories cheaper

297
00:09:51,456 --> 00:09:52,763
than China's?

298
00:09:52,843 --> 00:09:54,147
For some products,

299
00:09:54,147 --> 00:09:55,013
yes.

300
00:09:55,013 --> 00:09:57,419
The likeliest are heavy or bulky goods

301
00:09:57,419 --> 00:09:58,928
that are costly to ship,

302
00:09:58,928 --> 00:10:00,896
products built from many repeated

303
00:10:00,896 --> 00:10:01,968
precision steps,

304
00:10:01,968 --> 00:10:03,936
and things a country can't risk running

305
00:10:03,936 --> 00:10:04,570
out of,

306
00:10:04,570 --> 00:10:05,205
like chips,

307
00:10:05,205 --> 00:10:07,773
batteries and medical supplies.

308
00:10:07,903 --> 00:10:09,918
A toaster is light and cheap to

309
00:10:09,918 --> 00:10:10,396
ship,

310
00:10:10,396 --> 00:10:12,411
so Mel's choice is harder than she

311
00:10:12,411 --> 00:10:13,515
hoped.

312
00:10:13,750 --> 00:10:16,216
And if Mel does build in Ohio,

313
00:10:16,216 --> 00:10:18,709
her factory won't hire the crowds the

314
00:10:18,709 --> 00:10:20,140
old plants did.

315
00:10:20,330 --> 00:10:22,917
Economists Daron Acemoglu and Pascual

316
00:10:22,917 --> 00:10:25,505
Restrepo found that in American local job

317
00:10:25,505 --> 00:10:26,082
markets,

318
00:10:26,082 --> 00:10:27,654
the more robots arrived,

319
00:10:27,654 --> 00:10:29,645
the fewer people were employed and the

320
00:10:29,645 --> 00:10:31,240
lower wages went.

321
00:10:31,410 --> 00:10:32,980
The new jobs are different.

322
00:10:32,980 --> 00:10:33,961
Technicians,

323
00:10:33,961 --> 00:10:34,943
programmers,

324
00:10:34,943 --> 00:10:36,549
maintenance crews.

325
00:10:36,783 --> 00:10:37,771
Meanwhile,

326
00:10:37,771 --> 00:10:40,025
Wei's factory keeps its suppliers,

327
00:10:40,025 --> 00:10:40,759
its ports,

328
00:10:40,759 --> 00:10:41,747
its scale,

329
00:10:41,747 --> 00:10:43,773
and now the biggest fleet of new

330
00:10:43,773 --> 00:10:45,014
robots on Earth,

331
00:10:45,014 --> 00:10:47,133
most of them homemade.

332
00:10:47,433 --> 00:10:49,923
What could stop an American comeback?

333
00:10:50,073 --> 00:10:52,955
Robots sitting idle when demand is weak,

334
00:10:52,955 --> 00:10:54,384
missing parts suppliers,

335
00:10:54,384 --> 00:10:55,813
expensive money,

336
00:10:55,813 --> 00:10:58,350
and trade rules that keep changing.

337
00:10:58,617 --> 00:11:00,357
So here's our verdict.

338
00:11:00,357 --> 00:11:02,549
AI doesn't end China's manufacturing

339
00:11:02,549 --> 00:11:03,587
dominance.

340
00:11:03,587 --> 00:11:05,447
It changes the test.

341
00:11:05,477 --> 00:11:06,556
For decades,

342
00:11:06,556 --> 00:11:08,537
the question was who has the cheapest

343
00:11:08,537 --> 00:11:09,577
workers.

344
00:11:09,717 --> 00:11:11,741
Now it's who can combine intelligent

345
00:11:11,741 --> 00:11:12,531
machines,

346
00:11:12,531 --> 00:11:13,928
reliable energy,

347
00:11:13,928 --> 00:11:16,155
patient capital and a complete supply

348
00:11:16,155 --> 00:11:16,957
chain.

349
00:11:17,187 --> 00:11:19,967
America can win back competitiveness,

350
00:11:19,967 --> 00:11:21,147
product by product,

351
00:11:21,147 --> 00:11:23,237
without winning back the same jobs.

352
00:11:23,237 --> 00:11:25,277
And China can automate too.

353
00:11:25,447 --> 00:11:27,226
Mel's choice won't be decided by the

354
00:11:27,226 --> 00:11:28,597
cheapest labor.

355
00:11:28,597 --> 00:11:31,065
It'll be decided by the best system.

356
00:11:31,317 --> 00:11:31,937
Next time,

357
00:11:31,937 --> 00:11:34,717
we follow the money behind another hidden

358
00:11:34,717 --> 00:11:36,957
link of the world economy.

359
00:11:36,957 --> 00:11:38,788
This is The Hidden Economics.
